  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="classification">Sorting the Worlds</h2>
      <p>
        When we sorted the stars in <a href={subPath("3.2")}>§3.2</a>, thousands of seemingly different suns collapsed
        into a few clean families on a single chart. Planets reward the same treatment. Astronomers have now confirmed
        <strong>more than 6,300 planets</strong> around other stars — <strong>exoplanets</strong> — on top of the eight
        at home, and the way to tame that zoo is to plot two simple properties against each other: the planet's
        <strong>size</strong> (its radius, measured in Earth radii) and its <strong>orbital period</strong> (the length
        of its year, which also tells us how close it sits to its star, and so how hot it runs). On that chart,
        explored in the figure below, the crowd separates into neighbourhoods.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the top sit the <strong>gas giants</strong>, ten or more Earth radii across — but split into two camps.
        The <strong>cold gas giants</strong> orbit far out, like our Jupiter and Saturn. The <strong>hot
        Jupiters</strong>, by contrast, complete an orbit in just days, roasting against their stars; the very first
        planet found around a Sun-like star, <strong>51 Pegasi b in 1995</strong>, was one, and their existence was the
        first hint that planets can <em>move</em> after birth (<a href={subPath("4.3")}>§4.3</a>). Below them come the
        <strong>ice giants</strong> like Uranus and Neptune, then the great middle class our solar system entirely
        lacks: <strong>super-Earths and mini-Neptunes</strong>, worlds of one to four Earth radii that are the most
        common type of planet known. The chart's corners hold the exotics — <strong>lava worlds</strong> orbiting so
        tightly their rock stays molten, possible <strong>ocean worlds</strong> wrapped in global seas, and, rarest of
        all to catch, <strong>Earth-like</strong> planets on temperate, year-long orbits. Click each family in the
        figure; the arrow keys walk through them.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The planet zoo, sorted by size and orbit">
        <ClassMapPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        Size and orbit sketch the families, but to learn what a planet is <em>made of</em> we need a second chart. If
        we also know a planet's <strong>mass</strong>, then mass divided by volume gives its <strong>density</strong> —
        and density is a fingerprint of composition. Earth, a ball of rock and iron, packs
        <strong>5.5 grams into every cubic centimetre</strong> (five and a half times water's density). Saturn, a ball
        of hydrogen, manages only <strong>0.7 g/cm³</strong> — less dense than water, so it would float in an
        impossibly large bathtub. Those two values draw the reference lines in the figure below. A world hugging the
        rock line is terrestrial; a world near the Saturn line is gas; and the wide space between is where the
        in-between worlds live — ice giants, and super-Earths whose radius balloons if they keep even a thin
        hydrogen-helium envelope. How we actually weigh and measure such distant planets — the transit for the radius,
        the stellar wobble for the mass — is the whole story of <a href={subPath("4.4")}>§4.4</a>.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Mass, radius, and density">
        <MassRadiusPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <h2 id="worlds">Two Worlds Up Close</h2>
      <p>
        Statistics tell us what kinds of planets exist; visiting one tells us how a planet <em>works</em>. So let us
        end with two case studies from home — one from each end of the density chart. First, the lightweight champion:
        <strong>Saturn</strong>. The second-largest planet is a working scale model of a solar system. More than
        <strong>270 confirmed moons</strong> orbit it — a single 2025 announcement added 128 at once — from ice-ball
        <strong>Enceladus</strong>, whose south-pole geysers vent a buried ocean of liquid water into space, to mighty
        <strong>Titan</strong>, bigger than Mercury and the only moon anywhere with a dense atmosphere, complete with
        methane rain and rivers. And around it all spread the <strong>rings</strong>: countless orbiting chunks of
        nearly pure water ice, wider than the Earth–Moon distance yet in places only ten metres thick, their sharp
        edges and gaps patrolled by small <strong>shepherd moons</strong> whose gravity herds the ice into line — the
        same gravitational choreography, in miniature, that governs the planets around the Sun. Tour the system in the
        figure below with the arrow keys.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The Saturn system">
        <SaturnSystemPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        Now the heavyweight — and the only world known to be alive: <strong>Earth</strong>. Its secret is that it is
        still a <em>running machine</em>. Heat left over from its formation, topped up by radioactive decay, keeps the
        deep interior churning. That churning drives <strong>plate tectonics</strong>: the rigid crust is broken into
        plates that drift a few centimetres per year — about as fast as your fingernails grow — colliding, diving
        under one another, and being recycled through the mantle. Run the clock across a billion years and whole
        continents assemble and scatter: the supercontinent <strong>Rodinia</strong> formed around 1,000 million years
        ago and broke apart around 750; its successor <strong>Pangea</strong> gathered by about 335 million years ago
        and has been rifting apart since about 175 — the Atlantic Ocean is one of the cracks, still widening today.
        This endless recycling regulates the atmosphere and climate over geological time; a dead, still planet could
        not do it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same engine also builds Earth's defences. The churning liquid-iron <strong>outer core</strong> is a natural
        dynamo, generating a <strong>magnetic field</strong> that inflates a protective bubble far larger than the
        planet — reaching about ten Earth radii toward the Sun and streaming millions of kilometres downwind as a
        tail. This <strong>magnetosphere</strong> deflects the solar wind we met in <a href={subPath("4.1")}>§4.1</a>;
        particles that do get trapped collect in the doughnut-shaped <strong>Van Allen belts</strong>, and the few that
        leak down the polar field lines paint the <strong>auroras</strong>. Beneath the shield floats the layered
        <strong>atmosphere</strong>: the weather-filled troposphere, then the stratosphere whose <strong>ozone
        layer</strong> absorbs the Sun's harshest ultraviolet, the frigid mesosphere, and the thin ionosphere fading
        into space. Interior, shield, and air are one linked system — switch between them in the figure below. Without
        the dynamo, the wind would slowly strip the air; without the air, no oceans and no life — the thread
        chapter 5 will pick up.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Earth: interior, atmosphere, magnetic shield">
        <EarthPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <hr />

      <p><em>Six thousand worlds sort into a handful of families by size, orbit, and density — and our own two extremes,
        floating Saturn and living Earth, show what the numbers mean in the flesh. But why does the family a planet
        joins depend on where it was born? That is the question of formation — next.</em></p>
    </div>
  </article>

